ψ Metamaterials is the integration page for Branch B. It takes the material parameters established across B1–B8 — susceptibility, permittivity, permeability, loss tangent, quality factor, coherence length — and asks the question that defines the metamaterial design philosophy: what happens when these parameters are not fixed properties of a bulk material, but engineered quantities that arise from a precisely controlled geometric architecture?

The answer, established in electromagnetic metamaterials since Veselago (1968) and Smith et al. (2000), is that geometry at sub-wavelength scales can produce effective medium properties that no bulk material exhibits — negative permittivity, negative permeability, near-zero index, hyperbolic dispersion. The ψ equivalent is: geometry at sub-coherence-length scales can engineer ε_ψ, μ_ψ, and χ_ψ to values and combinations unreachable through bulk material selection alone.

== The ψ Metamaterial Design Philosophy ==

=== Geometry First, Chemistry Second ===

The central principle distinguishes ψ metamaterials from conventional composite materials:

* '''Conventional composite:''' Add BST nanoparticles to a polymer matrix. The resulting ε_ψ is determined by the volume fraction and the constituent permittivities via Maxwell-Garnett theory. The geometry — sphere, rod, platelet — matters only at second order.

* '''ψ Metamaterial:''' Design a TPMS unit cell with a specific topology (Gyroid, Schwarz-P, or other) and a specific density parameter <math>t</math>. The unit cell geometry selects which ψ modes are supported inside the structure, which are forbidden, and how they propagate. The chemical fill then fine-tunes the performance within the envelope the geometry establishes.

This distinction has a practical consequence: changing from a Gyroid to a Schwarz-P lattice at the same density and with identical fill produces a different ψ device — not a different formulation of the same device. The topology is a primary design variable.

=== The Three Geometry-Determined Parameters ===

From the material parameter definitions in [[ψ Constitutive Relations]] (B1):

* '''ε_ψ''' is controlled by the TPMS density parameter <math>t</math> (which sets the pore volume fraction and therefore the effective dielectric constant via Bruggeman theory) and by the BST bias voltage (fine-tuning via [[ψ Permittivity]] B2).

* '''μ_ψ''' is controlled by the ferrofluid microchannel routing geometry (which determines how the high-μ ferrofluid is spatially distributed relative to the ψ mode field lines) and by the applied H-field (fine-tuning via [[ψ Permeability]] B3).

* '''χ_ψ''' — the susceptibility — is the least directly controlled parameter, and the one most dependent on the Track B hypothesis. Within the established physics, χ_ψ of the composite is a consequence of ε_ψ and μ_ψ via the relation from B1: <math>\varepsilon_\psi = \varepsilon_0(1 + \chi_\psi)</math>.

== Effective Medium Theory for TPMS Geometries ==

=== Why Maxwell-Garnett Is Insufficient ===

The Maxwell-Garnett effective medium theory (used in [[Smart Composite Fill]] B8 for the BST prediction) assumes spherical inclusions embedded in a continuous matrix — one phase is the "host" and the other is the "guest." This assumption fails for TPMS geometries because both the solid scaffold and the pore volume are topologically continuous throughout the structure — there is no host and no guest. Both phases percolate.

The correct effective medium theory for bicontinuous structures is the '''Bruggeman symmetric medium''':

:<math>f_1\,\frac{\varepsilon_1 - \varepsilon_{\rm eff}}{\varepsilon_1 + 2\varepsilon_{\rm eff}} + f_2\,\frac{\varepsilon_2 - \varepsilon_{\rm eff}}{\varepsilon_2 + 2\varepsilon_{\rm eff}} = 0</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = Bruggeman symmetric effective medium equation
| equation       = <math>f_1\,\frac{\varepsilon_1 - \varepsilon_{\rm eff}}{\varepsilon_1 + 2\varepsilon_{\rm eff}} + f_2\,\frac{\varepsilon_2 - \varepsilon_{\rm eff}}{\varepsilon_2 + 2\varepsilon_{\rm eff}} = 0</math>
| in_english     =
* <math>f_1, f_2 = 1 - f_1</math> — volume fractions of the two phases (solid scaffold and composite fill).
* <math>\varepsilon_1, \varepsilon_2</math> — permittivities of the two phases. For TPMS: <math>\varepsilon_1</math> is the photopolymer scaffold; <math>\varepsilon_2</math> is the BST-PZT-quartz composite fill.
* <math>\varepsilon_{\rm eff}</math> — the effective permittivity of the bicontinuous composite. This is the unknown — solve the quadratic equation.
* '''Solved form:''' <math>\varepsilon_{\rm eff} = \frac{1}{4}\left[(3f_1-1)\varepsilon_1 + (3f_2-1)\varepsilon_2 + \sqrt{\Delta}\right]</math> where <math>\Delta = [(3f_1-1)\varepsilon_1 + (3f_2-1)\varepsilon_2]^2 + 8\varepsilon_1\varepsilon_2</math>
* '''Key difference from Maxwell-Garnett:''' Bruggeman predicts a percolation threshold at <math>f_1 = 1/3</math> — below this, the conductive phase does not percolate and the composite is insulating. Above it, conduction paths exist. Maxwell-Garnett has no percolation threshold. The TPMS density targets <math>t = 0.3</math>–<math>0.5</math> (scaffold fraction 18–37%) are chosen to stay near this threshold — maximising surface-to-volume ratio while maintaining structural percolation.
}}

=== Anisotropic Extension for TPMS ===

The Gyroid belongs to space group Ia3d — it has cubic symmetry but no mirror planes along certain directions. This produces a slightly anisotropic effective medium tensor even in the Bruggeman model. The correct extension replaces scalar ε_eff with the effective permittivity tensor:

:<math>[\varepsilon_{\rm eff}] = \begin{pmatrix}\varepsilon_\perp & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & \varepsilon_\perp & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & \varepsilon_\parallel\end{pmatrix}</math>

where <math>\varepsilon_\parallel</math> is enhanced along the symmetry axis after field-cure alignment (from [[Smart Composite Fill]] B8): <math>\varepsilon_\parallel/\varepsilon_\perp \approx 1.25</math>–<math>1.35</math>.

Full-wave FEM computation (COMSOL) is required to validate beyond the Bruggeman approximation — the TPMS geometry's complex curvature creates local field enhancement effects that scalar effective medium theories do not capture.

