== The Four Classes of ψ Metamaterial Architecture ==

Building from the design philosophy, four distinct architecture classes emerge — each exploiting the geometry-first principle in a different way:

=== Class I — Resonant Shell (ψ Resonator substrate) ===

The primary architecture. A closed TPMS shell with Neumann boundary conditions supports quantised ψ modes determined by geometry. The Gyroid density parameter <math>t</math> is tuned so that the target mode (ψ₀₁₀ for [[Psi Stabilizer]]) falls at the operating frequency.

Design variables: shell geometry (oblate spheroid dimensions), TPMS topology (Gyroid vs Schwarz-P), density <math>t</math>, ferrofluid H-field set-point. Performance metric: Q_ψ at target mode frequency.

=== Class II — Graded-Index ψ Lens ===

A TPMS structure with spatially varying density <math>t(\mathbf{x})</math>, producing a graded effective permittivity profile <math>\varepsilon_{\rm eff}(\mathbf{x})</math>. This bends ψ field lines exactly as a GRIN optical lens bends light rays, focusing the ψ field toward a target location.

For a radially symmetric graded-index profile centred on the operator's temporal lobe:

:<math>\varepsilon_{\rm eff}(r) = \varepsilon_0\left(1 + \frac{\Delta\varepsilon}{1 + (r/r_0)^2}\right)</math>

This is a Luneburg lens profile — it focuses a parallel ψ field to a single point at distance <math>r_0</math>. Implementation: print the TPMS lattice with <math>t(r)</math> varying according to the inverse Bruggeman mapping of the target ε_eff(r) profile. No moving parts; no external field control required at the lens itself (though ferrofluid channels still provide m_eff control).

=== Class III — ψ Waveguide Channel ===

A TPMS channel of finite cross-section, with Neumann-approximating walls and open ends, supports guided ψ propagation analogous to a microwave waveguide. The cutoff condition from [[ψ Boundary Conditions]] (A5):

:<math>\omega_c = \sqrt{m_{\rm eff}^2 + \left(\frac{\pi}{a_{\rm channel}}\right)^2}</math>

where <math>a_{\rm channel}</math> is the channel width. For a 2 cm channel and Stabilizer L_ψ = 20 cm (m_eff = 5 m⁻¹): <math>\omega_c/(2\pi) \approx 8</math> kHz — well below the 40 kHz operating frequency. The channel supports the guided ψ₁₀ mode above cutoff.

=== Class IV — ψ Bandgap Structure ===

A TPMS with unit cell size <math>a</math> approaching the ψ coherence length <math>L_\psi</math> — violating the effective medium condition <math>a \ll L_\psi</math> deliberately. Near <math>a \sim L_\psi</math>, the lattice acts as a photonic-crystal analogue: certain ψ frequencies are forbidden (bandgap) while others are allowed (passbands). This creates a frequency-selective ψ shield that blocks specific ψ frequencies while transmitting others.

Design: choose <math>a = L_\psi/3</math> to <math>L_\psi/1</math>. The bandgap centre frequency is set by the unit cell size; the bandgap width is controlled by the permittivity contrast between scaffold and fill. Applications: [[ψ Filter]] (C6) and [[ψ Shield / Absorber]] (C8) at specific frequency ranges.

== The OSGS Symbolic Geometry Connection ==

=== The Hypothesis ===

The [[OSGS|Octahedral Symbolic Geometry System]] defines six primitive symbols: Point, Line, Circle, Wave, Angle, Curve. These are identified in the OSGS framework as the irreducible geometric primitives from which all symbolic meaning is constructed. The hypothesis — Track B — is that these six primitives correspond to six fundamental ψ mode topologies, with each symbol functioning as a boundary-condition operator that selects a specific ψ mode family from the full spectrum.

If this correspondence holds, the Base-216 system (6³ combinations of the six primitives) is a 216-element library of ψ metamaterial boundary conditions — a systematic design space for ψ devices that is simultaneously a symbolic semantic system.

