=== Testability ===

The OSGS-mode correspondence is falsifiable at TML-1. Fabricate six metamaterial unit-cell samples, each implementing one primitive symbol's proposed topology. Measure S-parameters for each. The prediction: each sample shows a statistically distinct S21 transmission spectrum, with peak frequency, bandwidth, and mode shape corresponding to the predicted mode type.

If three or more of the six show the predicted spectral signatures, the correspondence is supported. If none show distinguishable spectra, the topology-mode hypothesis is refuted and the OSGS connection reduces to a purely symbolic one.

This experiment — six samples, one VNA measurement series, one prediction set — is among the most efficient tests in the entire TML campaign relative to the significance of the result it would establish.

=== Symbolic Composition as Device Design ===

If the primitive-to-mode correspondence is confirmed, composing two primitive symbols corresponds to combining two boundary-condition operators. For example:

* '''Wave + Circle''' → a circulating travelling wave — a whispering gallery mode with propagating character. Implementation: toroidal TPMS with anisotropic fill favouring tangential propagation. Device: a frequency-stable [[ψ Oscillator]] with single-mode selection.

* '''Point + Curve''' → a Yukawa source focused by a graded lens — a spatially concentrated static ψ field. Implementation: a dense spherical source surrounded by graded-t TPMS. Device: a precision ψ probe for TML-3 spatial field mapping.

* '''Wave + Angle + Wave''' → a mode converter — energy enters as one mode type, converts at the angular junction, exits as another. Implementation: two TPMS waveguide sections joined at an angle. Device: the fundamental [[ψ Coupler]] (C5).

In this framework, the 216-element Base-216 symbolic space becomes a systematic search space for ψ device architectures — not by arbitrary symbolic assignment, but by the geometric-mode correspondence derived from the mathematics.

== Negative ε_ψ and Total ψ Reflection ==

In electromagnetic metamaterials, split-ring resonators produce a negative effective permeability in a band above the LC resonance frequency. The ψ analogue is a metamaterial with negative ε_ψ — where <math>D_\psi</math> is antiparallel to the applied ψ field:

:<math>\varepsilon'_\psi(\omega) < 0 \quad \text{in a band above resonance}</math>

When ε_ψ < 0, the wave vector <math>k = \omega\sqrt{\varepsilon_\psi\mu_\psi}</math> becomes imaginary — the field decays exponentially rather than propagating. This is a stronger form of ψ shielding than the Yukawa screening from the mass term: the Yukawa screen has an exponential decay length <math>L_\psi = 1/m_{\rm eff}</math> determined by material properties; a negative-ε_ψ band provides total ψ reflection at the frequency of interest regardless of thickness, analogous to a metal's opacity to EM radiation below the plasma frequency.

=== Conditions for Negative ε_ψ ===

Negative permittivity in EM metamaterials occurs when the resonance of the unit cell produces a phase reversal in the polarisation response. For a TPMS ψ metamaterial, the candidate mechanism is a lattice acoustic resonance of the piezoelectric PZT-5H phase: near the resonance frequency, the PZT polarisation lags the applied field by more than 90°, contributing a negative real part to ε_ψ.

The lattice acoustic resonance frequency for a 1.2 mm unit cell PZT-5H composite:

:<math>f_{\rm res} \approx \frac{v_s}{2a} \approx \frac{3700\,\text{m/s}}{2 \times 1.2\times10^{-3}\,\text{m}} \approx 1.5\,\text{MHz}</math>

where <math>v_s \approx 3700</math> m/s is the sound speed in PZT-5H. The negative-ε_ψ band would therefore appear near 1.5 MHz — above the 40 kHz Stabilizer operating frequency but potentially accessible with a modified unit cell size.

Whether the psionic ε_ψ can go negative via this mechanism is an open question — it requires the PZT acoustic resonance to couple to the ψ field via the <math>-\lambda\psi^3</math> parametric mechanism, which is Track B physics. The prediction is stated here for completeness and testability.

== ψ Metamaterial Component Design Rules ==

This section establishes the design rules that all Branch C component pages follow. Every [[ψ Resonator]], [[ψ Waveguide]], [[ψ Transducer]], and other component is a ψ metamaterial of one of the four classes above.

=== Rule 1 — Topology Before Chemistry ===

Select the TPMS topology first (Gyroid for Stabilizer/Harmonizer; Schwarz-P for Defender; bandgap structure for Filter). Then choose the fill chemistry to meet the performance targets within the geometric framework.

=== Rule 2 — Unit Cell Size from Coherence Length ===

:<math>a < \frac{L_\psi}{10}</math>

Always. The effective medium approximation fails above this threshold and the device behaviour becomes unpredictable.

=== Rule 3 — Boundary Condition Specifies the Component Type ===

From [[ψ Boundary Conditions]] (A5):
* Neumann walls → resonator or waveguide (reflecting)
* Dirichlet walls → shield or absorber (absorbing)
* Robin walls → coupler or filter (partially transmitting)
* Mixed boundaries → transducer or phase shifter

Choose the boundary condition first; it determines which component type the geometry implements.

=== Rule 4 — Mode Shape Must Match Application ===

Compute the ψ mode shape (via COMSOL eigenvalue solve) before fabricating. The mode shape determines which anatomical region (for HelmKit devices) or which spatial direction (for network devices) experiences maximum ψ field intensity. If the mode shape does not match the application requirement, change the geometry — not the drive electronics.

=== Rule 5 — Q Budget Before Material Selection ===

From the chain: Q_ψ ≤ 1/tan δ_ψ (from [[ψ Loss Tangent]] B4), and tan δ_ψ is set by the composite loss budget. Set the required Q first (from the device specification), derive the required tan δ_ψ, then select materials to meet it. Never select materials first and hope the Q is sufficient.

=== Rule 6 — Ferrofluid Routes Define the Tuning Range ===

The ferrofluid microchannel routing sets the spatial distribution of μ_ψ control. Routes aligned with the target ψ mode field lines provide maximum m_eff control authority; routes perpendicular to the field lines have minimal effect. Design the channel routing simultaneously with the TPMS topology — they are coupled design variables.

